WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.600
How do you arrange rectangular circuit blocks inside a tight chip boundary perfectly, without getting trapped in overlapping chaos or bad local guesses?

2
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.000
That’s simulated annealing floorplan search.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Take this toy floorplanning layout with modules A through E.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:24.900
To separate great floorplans from invalid layouts, we define a cost function that heavily penalizes illegality.

5
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:33.000
Our cost model adds a massive 1,000-point penalty tax whenever a packing is illegal.

6
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.000
The energy landscape: bad seed versus golden packing.

7
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:43.800
The bad overflow seed sits at a cost of about 1,044, dominated almost entirely by that 1,000-point penalty tax. But here is the goal.

8
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:53.000
The golden legal packing drops below the penalty floor to just 36, consisting purely of deadspace and a small wirelength proxy with zero illegality tax.

9
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:59.545
Annealing must navigate this steep energy landscape to move from illegal chaos to legal perfection.

10
00:00:59.545 --> 00:01:03.036
Execution loop and the Metropolis criterion.

11
00:01:03.036 --> 00:01:05.873
So how does annealing find the global optimum?

12
00:01:05.873 --> 00:01:17.436
In the pseudocode execution loop, the algorithm proposes neighbor moves, such as an saSwap that exchanges module origin coordinates, and evaluates them using the Metropolis criterion.

13
00:01:17.436 --> 00:01:29.000
At high initial temperatures T, worsening moves are accepted with probability P = e^(-Δ C / T), allowing the search to escape bad local seeds.

14
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:38.000
As the temperature cools down, cost falls below 1,000 and legality flips to true — exactly what a proper cooling schedule should prefer.

15
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.216
Representation and topology edits.

16
00:01:42.216 --> 00:01:46.667
But here is the friction: annealing is only as good as its move set.

17
00:01:46.667 --> 00:02:04.000
Simple coordinate swapping serves as a starting point, but pairing annealing with B*-trees, sequence pairs, or soft-module aspect-ratio edits — like reshaping module A from 3×2 to 2×3 — unlocks true structural flexibility in advanced VLSI layouts.

18
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:12.611
In your implementation, watch out for these common traps: optimizing deadspace while accepting illegal states without penalty:

19
00:02:12.611 --> 00:02:17.889
forgetting to rescore and re-evaluate placement cost immediately after a swap

20
00:02:17.889 --> 00:02:24.000
cooling so fast (quenching) that you never escape the initial bad seed.

21
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.994
Now it’s your turn in the lab.

22
00:02:25.994 --> 00:02:37.560
Open simulated-annealing-fp, demonstrate one improve step from bad to golden, match the starter metrics, take the quiz, and report your final parts, cost, and legality.

23
00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:42.036
Imagine trying to pack heavy wooden blocks into a display box in complete darkness.

24
00:02:42.036 --> 00:02:49.732
If a single block spills over the edge or overlaps another, a strict referee hits you with a massive 1,000-point penalty fine.

25
00:02:49.732 --> 00:02:53.580
That is the core challenge of simulated annealing floorplan search.

26
00:02:53.580 --> 00:03:05.080
In physical chip layout design, simulated annealing (SA) optimizes module placement by balancing spatial metrics while strictly outlawing illegal overlaps or boundary spills.

27
00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:17.330
Under our cost model, a messy initial overflow seed sits at an unbearable cost near 1,044, whereas a golden legal packing drops down to just 36.

28
00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:24.580
Simulated annealing naturally guides us out of that high-penalty chaos toward legal, low-cost layout perfection.

29
00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:40.580
To separate great floorplans from bad ones, the algorithm relies on a clear composite cost function: cost = C_deadspace + C_HPWL + C_legality This function heavily penalizes any illegality.

30
00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:49.232
In our pseudocode workflow, we start with an initial placement S0, a high starting temperature T0, and a steady cooling rate alpha.

31
00:03:49.232 --> 00:04:04.176
As long as temperature T stays above T_min, the algorithm explores neighboring layouts using saSwap, computes ΔC = cost(S_candidate) − cost(S_current), and tests the Metropolis acceptance rule.

32
00:04:04.176 --> 00:04:11.647
If a candidate placement is accepted and proves to be legal and lower in cost than S_best, we update S_best.

33
00:04:11.647 --> 00:04:15.580
Then we cool down with T = T × alpha.

34
00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:19.001
Let’s look at our two benchmark states side by side.

35
00:04:19.001 --> 00:04:37.019
On one side sits the bad overflow seed, marked false for legality due to overlapping modules and boundary spills, triggering that brutal +1,000 penalty tax on top of a deadspace + HPWL score of about 44, bringing total cost to approximately 1,044.

36
00:04:37.019 --> 00:04:47.966
On the other side sits our golden legal packing, marked true for legality, paying a penalty tax of 0, and keeping deadspace + HPWL down to about 36.

37
00:04:47.966 --> 00:04:54.580
With just one smart teaching step, we swap out the chaotic seed for the golden legal packing.

38
00:04:54.580 --> 00:05:01.852
Examining our three cost terms in detail: C_deadspace measures wasted empty room inside the bounding box:

39
00:05:01.852 --> 00:05:06.543
C_HPWL acts as a half-perimeter wirelength proxy measuring routing distance

40
00:05:06.543 --> 00:05:13.580
and C_legality slams down a +1,000 tax the moment an overlap or overflow occurs.

41
00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:22.580
Phase 1 opens at the bad overflow seed with a score of 1,044.4, dominated by the +1,000 penalty.

42
00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:34.687
In phase 2, we trigger our perturbation move engine, saSwap, which selects two modules at random and exchanges their lower-left (x, y) origin coordinates, while keeping their dimensions fixed.

43
00:05:34.687 --> 00:05:44.593
Under the Metropolis rule, helpful moves with ΔC ≤ 0 are accepted immediately, whereas worsening moves with ΔC > 0 are accepted with probability P_accept = e^(-Δ C / T).

44
00:05:44.593 --> 00:05:57.580
High temperatures give the algorithm freedom to escape bad seeds early on, while lower temperatures cool the solution into an optimal local minimum.

45
00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:04.097
In phase 3, executing saSwap on the conflicting modules removes the +1,000 penalty tax.

46
00:06:04.097 --> 00:06:17.580
Total cost falls below 1,000 to 35.7, reflecting deadspace and an HPWL proxy of 12.5, and legality flips to true, which is exactly what a cooling schedule should prefer.

47
00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:26.516
Of course, an annealing search is only as effective as the quality of its move set and spatial representation.

48
00:06:26.516 --> 00:06:41.580
While origin-coordinate swapping provides a simple baseline, production floorplanners pair annealing with B*-tree or sequence-pair representations to guarantee zero overlap by construction, alongside soft-module sizing and macro placement in the labs that follow.

49
00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:49.672
Be careful to avoid three critical implementation pitfalls: accepting illegal states without applying the +1,000 penalty tax:

50
00:06:49.672 --> 00:06:56.500
forgetting to rescore and re-evaluate cost(S_candidate) immediately after an saSwap move

51
00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:03.580
cooling temperature T too rapidly (quenching), which traps the algorithm inside the initial bad seed.

52
00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:14.521
Demonstrate one improve step transitioning the layout from the bad overflow seed to the golden legal packing, bringing total cost below 1,000 and restoring legality to true.

53
00:07:14.521 --> 00:07:23.230
Next, we will extend our move set to include soft-module reshaping, flexing soft module A from a 3×2 to a 2×3 layout.
